WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.400
Creating high-quality photorealistic materials
for light transport simulations typically

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.990
includes direct hands-on interaction with
a principled shader.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:14.180
This means that the user has to tweak a large
number of material properties by hand, and

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.089
has to wait for a new image of it to be rendered
after each interaction.

00:00:18.089 --> 00:00:23.260
This requires a fair bit expertise and the
best setups are often obtained through a lengthy

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.140
trial and error process.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:30.909
To enhance this workflow, we present a learning-based
system for rapid mass-scale material synthesis.

00:00:30.909 --> 00:00:36.210
First, the user is presented with a gallery
of materials and the assigned scores are shown

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.440
in the upper left.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.520
Here, we learn the concept of glassy and transparent
materials.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.060
By leaning on only a few tens of high-scoring
samples, our system is able to recommend many

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.750
new materials from the learned distributions.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.870
The learning step typically takes a few seconds,
where the recommendations take negligible

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.820
time and can be done on a mass scale.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.220
Then, these recommendations can be used to
populate a scene with materials.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:06.630
Typically, each recommendation takes 40-60
seconds to render with global illumination,

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:11.700
which is clearly unacceptable for real-world
workflows for even mid-sized galleries.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.630
In the next step, we propose a convolutional
neural network that is able to predict images

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:21.659
of these materials that are close to the ones
generated via global illumination, and takes

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:24.020
less than 3 milliseconds per image.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:29.270
Sometimes a recommended material is close
the one envisioned by the user, but requires

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.270
a bit of fine-tuning.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:35.929
To this end, we embed our high-dimensional
shader descriptors into an intuitive 2D latent

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:40.869
space where exploration and adjustments can
take place without any domain expertise.

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:45.889
However, this isn't very useful without additional
information because the user does not know

00:01:45.889 --> 00:01:51.060
which regions offer useful material models
that are in line with their scores.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.069
One of our key observations is that this latent
space technique can be combined with Gaussian

00:01:56.069 --> 00:02:00.979
Process Regression to provide an intuitive
color coding of the expected preferences to

00:02:00.979 --> 00:02:04.030
help highlighting the regions that may be
of interest.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:08.850
Furthermore, our convolutional neural network
can also provide real-time predictions of

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:09.920
these images.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.960
These predictions are close to indistinguishable
from the real rendered images and are generated

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.000
in real time.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.720
Beyond the preference map, this neural network
also opens up the possibility of visualizing

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.900
the expected similarity of these new materials
to the one we seek to fine-tune.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.640
By combining the preference and similarity
maps, we obtain a color coding that guides

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.320
the user in this latent space towards materials
that are both similar and have a high expected

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.360
score.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.800
To accentuate the utility of our real-time
variant generation technique, we show a practical

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:00.940
case where one of the grape materials is almost
done, but requires a slight reduction in vividity.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.680
This adjustment doesn't require any domain
expertise or direct interaction with a material

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.200
modeling system and can be done in real time.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.930
In this example, we learn the concept of translucent
materials from only a handful of high-scoring

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.600
samples and generate a large amount of recommendations
from the learned distribution.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:47.360
These recommendations can then be used to
populate a scene with relevant materials.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:53.600
Here, we show the preference and similarity
maps of the learned translucent material space

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.400
and explore possible variants of an input
material.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.000
These recommendations can be used for mass-scale
material synthesis, and the amount of variation

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.760
can be tweaked to suit the user's artistic
vision.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:25.090
After assigning the appropriate materials,
displacements and other advanced effects can

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.900
be easily added to these materials.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.120
We have also experimented with an extended,
more expressive version of our shader that

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.040
also includes procedural textured albedos
and displacements.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:44.320
The following scenes were populated using
the material learning and recommendation and

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:45.600
latent space embedding steps.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.800
We have proposed a system for mass-scale material
synthesis that is able to rapidly recommend

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:57.790
a broad range of new material models after
learning the user preferences from a modest

00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:59.100
number of samples.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.730
Beyond this pipeline, we also explored powerful
combinations of the three used learning algorithms,

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:10.880
thereby opening up the possibility of real-time
photorealistic material visualization, exploration

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.620
and fine-tuning in a 2D latent space.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:19.310
We believe this feature set offers a useful
solution for rapid mass-scale material synthesis

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:24.760
for novice and expert users alike and hope
to see more exploratory works combining the

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:28.860
advantages of multiple state of the art learning
algorithms in the future.

